-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:3.2rem 1.5rem">
-<p style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The honest version</p>
-<h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Myrtle Beach cost of living: lower, but not as low as you expect</h2>
-<p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Here&rsquo;s something we say to almost every retiree moving down from the Northeast: your money goes further here, but not as far as you think it does. We&rsquo;ve watched people come from Boston and New York expecting third-world prices, and yes, the taxes and day-to-day costs are dramatically lower than up north. The problem is what happens next. People celebrate the savings by spending like crazy, and we&rsquo;ve seen folks end up <em>poorer</em> here than they were before they moved. The Grand Strand hands you a real head start. The retirees who win are the ones who protect it instead of blowing it in the first year.</p>
-</div></section>
-
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:3.2rem 1.5rem">
-<p style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Why it&rsquo;s cheaper here</p>
-<h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What lowers your bills in South Carolina</h2>
-<p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina is one of the more tax-friendly states in the country for retirees, and the difference from the Northeast is not small:</p>
-<ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.8;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem;padding-left:1.2rem">
-<li><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Social Security isn&rsquo;t taxed by the state</strong> at all.</li>
-<li><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Other retirement income gets a deduction</strong>, and military retirement pay is fully exempt from South Carolina income tax, regardless of age or income level.</li>
-<li><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Property taxes are low</strong>, homes you live in are assessed at the state&rsquo;s lowest ratio, and at 65+ the homestead exemption knocks the first slice of your home&rsquo;s value off the tax rolls entirely.</li>
-</ul>
-<p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Put those together and the monthly &ldquo;cost of just owning the house&rdquo; is often a fraction of what a comparable home cost you up north.</p>
-</div></section>
-
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:3.2rem 1.5rem">
-<p style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">For veterans</p>
-<h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">South Carolina veteran benefits: tax exemptions and tuition</h2>
-<p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you&rsquo;re a veteran, South Carolina has perks that surprise people. Military retirement pay is exempt from state income tax, but the one that surprises people is education. South Carolina offers <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">free tuition at its state colleges to the children of qualifying wartime veterans</strong>. We&rsquo;ve had clients whose kids now attend Coastal Carolina University tuition-free because of it.</p>
-<p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Wartime service alone does not qualify. The veteran must also have died in service or of an illness or injury caused by service, been a prisoner of war or missing in action, or be rated permanently and totally disabled. A Medal of Honor or a Purple Heart for combat wounds also qualifies. Residency rules apply, and the child must be 26 or younger. Confirm the details with the SC Department of Veterans&rsquo; Affairs. But for the right family, it&rsquo;s a benefit worth tens of thousands of dollars that most people never hear about before they move.</p>
-</div></section>
-
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:3.2rem 1.5rem">
-<p style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">What surprises retirees who move here?</p>
-<h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Flood insurance and the other costs retirees underestimate</h2>
-<p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Flood insurance.</strong> Near the coast, your flood zone matters more than your street address, and the wrong zone raises your monthly cost. We check the zone before you ever write an offer, and it helps to know a lender here will usually only require hazard insurance, plus flood insurance if the home sits in a flood zone.</p>
-<p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">HOA fees.</strong> South Carolina has a <em>lot</em> of HOAs, and some of them overcharge badly for basic amenities. We&rsquo;ve seen HOA dues run over <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">$2,000 a month</strong>. A beautiful condo with a high HOA fee can cost you more than a bigger house down the road. Before you make an offer we pull the HOA&rsquo;s bylaws, budget, reserve study, and as much of the financials as we can get, and go through them with you.</p>
-</div></section>
-
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:3.2rem 1.5rem">
-<p style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">A real example</p>
-<h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What the move can look like</h2>
-<p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A couple sold their home in New York for around $700,000 and bought a <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">bigger</strong> home here on the coast for about $400,000, more house for far less money. On taxes and insurance alone, they now save over <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">$20,000 a year</strong>. Their kids attend Coastal Carolina University tuition-free under the state&rsquo;s program for qualifying veterans&rsquo; children. That&rsquo;s one family&rsquo;s numbers, not a promise, but it&rsquo;s the kind of math that makes people wish they&rsquo;d moved sooner.</p>
-<p style="margin-bottom:0"><a href="/buyers/relocating/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Relocating to the Grand Strand &rarr;</a> &nbsp; <a href="/buyers/second-home/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Buying a second home &rarr;</a> &nbsp; <a href="/submarkets/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Compare submarkets &rarr;</a></p>
-</div></section>
-
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Where retirees settle</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Where retirees settle on the Grand Strand</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The largest concentrations of 55+ communities are in <a href="/submarkets/conway/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Conway</a> and the Carolina Forest and northern <a href="/submarkets/myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Myrtle Beach</a> area: newer neighborhoods, single-level floor plans, and the lowest prices for the space you get. Community-by-community prices and fees: <a href="/buyers/55-plus-communities/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">55+ communities in Myrtle Beach</a> and <a href="/buyers/golf-communities/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">golf course communities</a>. Four have a full guide of their own: <a href="/buyers/55-plus-communities/del-webb-grande-dunes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Del Webb at Grande Dunes</a>, <a href="/buyers/55-plus-communities/del-webb-north-myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Del Webb North Myrtle Beach</a>, <a href="/buyers/55-plus-communities/myrtle-trace/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Myrtle Trace</a> in Conway and <a href="/buyers/55-plus-communities/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Seasons at Prince Creek West</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">But a 55+ community is not the automatic answer. Most of the area has no age restriction, and some of our retiree buyers are happier in <a href="/submarkets/pawleys-island/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Pawleys Island</a> or <a href="/submarkets/murrells-inlet/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Murrells Inlet</a>: more lively, on the water, walkable dinners on the MarshWalk instead of a clubhouse calendar. The right answer depends on how you want to spend a Tuesday.</p><div style="margin-top:1.4rem;display:flex;gap:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Talk through where you fit</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></section>
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div style="max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;padding:3.5rem 1.5rem"><p style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem">Before you file the change</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Check how your pension is taxed before you move</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina is kind to retirees, but not identically kind to every pension. New York does not tax its state and local government pensions; South Carolina taxes them past its deductions. New Jersey excludes up to $100,000 of retirement income for many couples at 62 and older; the equivalent here is smaller. Pennsylvania taxes no retirement income at all, so a Pennsylvania retiree&#39;s income tax goes up a little here, not down.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The property tax and housing gap usually outweighs all of it, but that is a calculation, not an assumption. We wrote the state by state numbers out in full: moving from <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-north-carolina/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">North Carolina</a>, <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-new-york/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">New York</a>, <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-new-jersey/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">New Jersey</a>, <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-pennsylvania/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Pennsylvania</a> and <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-ohio/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Ohio</a>. The <a href="/buyers/relocating/moving-checklist/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">new resident checklist</a> has the deadlines that follow the move.</p></div></section>
