ass="t">$1,290 tax/yr</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 3h14a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2zM12 7.5v9M7.5 12h9"/></svg></span><span><strong>Hospital</strong><span class="t">3 to 10 min</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated June 12, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Retiring here works best if you live here most of the year: no state tax on Social Security, and $1,290 a year in tax on a $400,000 house at 65.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Myrtle Beach is a good place to retire if you will live here most of the year. A retired couple on Social Security and a modest pension can owe no South Carolina income tax, and a $400,000 house you live in is taxed about $1,290 a year at 65 with the $50,000 exemption.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The low tax is not automatic. You apply for it. The same house kept as a second home is taxed about $4,820 a year.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">We price your year here before you buy.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Price my year here</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#good-place-to-retire" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Good place to retire?</a></li><li><a href="#yearly-cost" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Yearly cost at 65</a></li><li><a href="#income-tax" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Income tax</a></li><li><a href="#communities" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">55+ communities</a></li><li><a href="#hospitals" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Hospitals</a></li><li><a href="#hurricanes" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Hurricanes and flooding</a></li><li><a href="#weather" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Weather</a></li><li><a href="#retiring-to-myrtle-beach-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Retiring to Myrtle Beach FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="good-place-to-retire"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Good place to retire?">Is Myrtle Beach a good place to retire?</h2><style>main#main .c3-glance li{line-height:1.4}main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2){padding:.9rem 0 1.4rem!important}main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2)>.wrap{display:block!important;text-align:center!important;background:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(42,48,64,.96),rgba(28,32,40,.98))!important;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14)!important;border-radius:28px!important;padding:2.2rem 1.6rem!important}main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2)>.wrap>h2{color:var(--ivory)!important}main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2)>.wrap>p{color:rgba(244,239,232,.82)!important}@media (max-width:699px){main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2)>.wrap{padding:1.6rem 1.2rem 1.7rem!important;border-radius:24px!important}main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2)>.wrap>h2{font-size:1.5rem!important;line-height:1.25}}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, for most people who make it their full-time home. Find the line that fits you. Each one is explained further down the page.</p><style>main#main .c3-decide{list-style:none;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem;padding:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(17rem,1fr));gap:.75rem;max-width:880px}main#main .c3-decide li{margin:0;padding:1rem 1.05rem 1.05rem;border-radius:18px;background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.78),rgba(255,255,255,.55));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9),0 10px 28px -16px rgba(28,32,40,.35)}main#main .c3-decide .q{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.6rem;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.4;margin:0 0 .45rem}main#main .c3-decide .q .i{flex:none;display:flex;width:2.3rem;height:2.3rem;border-radius:50%;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:var(--brass-ink);background:rgba(196,120,58,.12)}main#main .c3-decide .a{color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0}@media (prefers-reduced-transparency:reduce){main#main .c3-decide li{background:#fbf8f3}}</style><ul class="c3-decide" role="list"><li><p class="q"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span><span>If you will live here most of the year</span></p><p class="a">Apply for the 4 percent rate. At 65, the $50,000 exemption brings the tax on a $400,000 house down to about $1,290 a year.</p></li><li><p class="q"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span><span>If it will be a second home for a few years first</span></p><p class="a">The same house is taxed about $4,820 a year until it becomes your legal home.</p></li><li><p class="q"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><span>If your income is Social Security and a modest pension</span></p><p class="a">Many households like this owe no South Carolina income tax at all. The examples below show three.</p></li><li><p class="q"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><span>If your pension is from another state's government</span></p><p class="a">South Carolina taxes it past its deductions. In our example below, a single person with a New York public pension owes about $560.</p></li><li><p class="q"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 3h14a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2zM12 7.5v9M7.5 12h9"/></svg></span><span>If you want a hospital close by</span></p><p class="a">Each of the four 55+ communities below is 3 to 10 minutes by car from a hospital.</p></li><li><p class="q"><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 8.5h10.5a3 3 0 1 0-3-3M3 12.5h15a3 3 0 1 1-3 3M3 16.5h6"/></svg></span><span>If you would rather not plan a hurricane evacuation</span></p><p class="a">Three of the four 55+ communities are outside every evacuation zone.</p></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The pros and cons are in the table. The cons come down to two things: a second home pays far more tax, and one of the four 55+ communities is inside a hurricane evacuation zone. The other costs people miss are <a href="#yearly-cost" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">below</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="yearly-cost"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Yearly cost at 65">What does a house here cost a year at 65?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A $400,000 house you live in costs about $1,380 a year in county tax and fees at 65, before insurance and any HOA fee. That is in the county outside the city limits, where Myrtle Trace is.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Property tax a year on one $400,000 house outside the cities"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Property tax a year on one $400,000 house outside the cities</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">How the house is used, why</span><span role="columnheader">tax a year</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">You live in it, 65 or older</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">4 percent rate and the $50,000 exemption</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.267" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1,290</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">You live in it, under 65</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">4 percent rate, no school operating tax</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.305" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1,470</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A second home or a rental</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">6 percent rate and the full school tax</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$4,820</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Horry County's 2026 tax rates, in the county outside the city limits, where Myrtle Trace is. Inside a city, the city's tax is added.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A house you live in is taxed on 4 percent of its value and pays no school operating tax. Every other house is taxed on 6 percent and pays it. At 65, the first $50,000 of your home's value is not taxed at all, and for 2026 only an owner who already qualified in 2025 gets $75,000.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">At 65, a $400,000 house you live in</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">A year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Property tax, with the $50,000 exemption</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$1,290</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Stormwater fee, on the same bill</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$89.40</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Total tax and fees</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$1,380</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">HOA fee, if it has one ($95 to $445 a month in the four 55+ communities)</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$0 to $5,340</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Flood insurance, if you want it</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$550 to $600</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Homeowners insurance</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">We get three quotes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside a city, the city's tax is added. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax calculator</a> has every town's rate.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The costs people miss</h3><ol style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Moving in late still counts.</strong> A house that becomes your legal home for any part of the year gets the 4 percent rate for the whole year. The exemption at 65 counts for the whole year too, if you qualify.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The 4 percent rate is not automatic.</strong> Until you apply, the house is billed at 6 percent, like a second home. Apply as soon as you close.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>You give up your old state.</strong> To get the 4 percent rate, you certify that no one in your household claims to be a legal resident of another state for any purpose.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The exemption at 65 is its own application.</strong> You must have lived in South Carolina for a year and turned 65 by December 31 of the year before. Apply at the <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/auditor/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">county auditor's office</a>, and inside a city at the city too, by July 15 for that year's bill.</li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Property tax up north can be many times this. One of our clients from New York sold a $1 million house with more than $20,000 a year in property tax. They bought a $700,000 house here, with about $3,200 in tax. That is one household, not a promise.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Your year here, priced before you offer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">For each house you like, we work out the property tax at its price, the HOA fee and the flood zone, and we get three insurance quotes.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Price my year here</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="income-tax"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Income tax">Does South Carolina tax Social Security and pensions?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina does not tax Social Security. Under its new rules, most retirees with modest incomes owe little or no state income tax.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Three example households under the 2026 rules</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col" class="n">Social Security a year</th><th scope="col">Other income a year</th><th scope="col" class="n">State income tax</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Married, both 66</th><td class="n" data-label="Social Security a year">$40,000</td><td data-label="Other income a year">$30,000 private pension</td><td class="n" data-label="State income tax">$0</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Single, 67</th><td class="n" data-label="Social Security a year">$24,000</td><td data-label="Other income a year">$50,000 New York public pension</td><td class="n" data-label="State income tax">about $560</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Married, 60 and 58</th><td class="n" data-label="Social Security a year">None</td><td data-label="Other income a year">$36,000 military retirement pay</td><td class="n" data-label="State income tax">$0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The rules behind those numbers:</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Social Security</strong> is not taxed.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Pensions, IRAs and 401(k) plans:</strong> each person deducts up to $3,000 a year before 65, and up to $10,000 from the year they turn 65. Government pensions from any state count.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>At 65,</strong> each person also gets $15,000 to deduct from any income, less their own retirement deduction. The two do not stack. A couple who are both 65 or older can deduct up to $30,000.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Military retirement pay</strong> is deducted in full, at any age.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>South Carolina's own deduction</strong> replaces the federal one: $30,000 for a married couple and $15,000 for a single person. It shrinks once income passes $80,000 for a couple or $40,000 for one person.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The tax</strong> is 1.99 percent on the first $30,000 of taxable income. Above that, it is 5.21 percent of the whole amount, minus $966.</li></ul><div class="c3-retcalc c3-calc-box" style="background:var(--navy);background-image:linear-gradient(160deg