+          <h1 class="news-title"><span class="field field--name-title field--type-string field--label-hidden">Estimated Income Tax payments due September 15? Read Information Letter #26-20 first.</span>
+</h1>
+    
+          <p class="news-date">Tuesday, Sep 1, 2026</p>
+    
+           
+            <div class="clearfix text-formatted field field--name-body field--type-text-with-summary field--label-hidden field__item"><p>If you’re required to make South Carolina estimated Income Tax payments, make sure you read&nbsp;<a href="/sites/dor/files/policies/IL26-20.pdf"><strong>Information Letter #26-20</strong></a> to understand how recent legislation impacts your 2026 payments.</p><p>Not sure if you are required to make estimated payments? Keep reading to learn more.</p><h4>What is <a href="/sites/dor/files/policies/IL26-20.pdf"><strong>Information Letter #26-20</strong></a>?</h4><ul><li>The SCDOR published <a href="/sites/dor/files/policies/IL26-20.pdf"><strong>Information Letter #26-20</strong></a> on Monday to help taxpayers understand the impacts of <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/sess126_2025-2026/bills/4216.htm"><strong>Act 110</strong></a> of the 2026 legislative session. &nbsp;<ul><li>Act 110 amends the state’s Income Tax structure and is effective starting with the 2026 tax year. Tax year 2026 returns aren’t due until April 15, 2027.</li></ul></li><li><a href="/sites/dor/files/policies/IL26-20.pdf"><strong>Information Letter #26-20</strong></a> offers guidance now for taxpayers making estimated payments, who may need to make adjustments before their September 15 payment.<ul><li>Be sure to take your April 15 and June 15 estimated payments into account when making your September 15 estimated payment.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>What has changed?&nbsp;</h4><p>Review <a href="/sites/dor/files/policies/IL26-20.pdf"><strong>Information Letter #26-20</strong></a> for complete details. Changes include: &nbsp;</p><ul><li>South Carolina now has two Individual Income Tax brackets, with modified tax rates.</li><li>South Carolina has decoupled from the federal deductions listed in Internal Revenue Code Sections 63(b) through (g). As a result, the calculation for South Carolina taxable income begins with federal adjusted gross income (AGI) instead of federal taxable income. &nbsp;</li><li>South Carolina also has a new South Carolina Income Adjusted Deduction (SCIAD), which replaces the federal standard deduction. The SCIAD is based on a taxpayer’s filing status and may be reduced based on a taxpayer’s federal AGI.<ul><li>See the formula in <a href="/sites/dor/files/policies/IL26-20.pdf"><strong>Information Letter #26-20</strong></a> for help in determining your SCIAD. The SCDOR also will launch a SCIAD calculator on MyDORWAY later this year.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Should I make estimated payments?</h4><ul><li>You must make estimated payments if you expect to owe an Income Tax liability of $100 or more when filing your South Carolina Individual Income Tax return.<ul><li>Use the 2026 Estimated Tax Worksheet on the <a href="/sites/dor/files/forms/SC1040ES_2026.pdf"><strong>Individual Declaration of Estimated Tax (SC1040ES)</strong></a> if you’re unsure if you need to make estimated payments.</li><li>Check out our <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yt97Z2j424M"><strong>Tax Tips video</strong></a> for the basics of estimated payments.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>How do I make estimated payments?</h4><ul><li>The easiest way to make your estimated payment is using MyDORWAY, at <a href="/pay"><strong>dor.sc.gov/pay</strong></a>. Select <strong>Individual Income Tax Payment</strong> to get started.</li><li>Or, you can make estimated payments using the voucher printed on the SC1040ES. &nbsp;</li></ul><h4>What if I haven’t filed my 2025 return?</h4><ul><li>Act 110 does <strong>not </strong>impact 2025 Income Tax returns, which are due October 15, 2026 after the SCDOR granted an automatic filing extension to <a href="/news/scdor-statement-income-tax-conformity-april-15-filing-deadline-extended-sc-returns" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="c7b34aa3-42b6-4bbb-857c-458fcb18cb4c" data-entity-substitution="canonical" title="SCDOR statement on Income Tax conformity; April 15 filing deadline extended for SC returns"><strong>all taxpayers in March</strong></a>. &nbsp;</li></ul><h4>Stay connected</h4><blockquote><p>All SCDOR Advisory Opinions are available at <a href="/policy" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="e996d2cd-7fdf-4708-9fab-d1b2c005c820" data-entity-substitution="canonical" title="Policy"><strong>dor.sc.gov/policy</strong></a>. You can have Advisory Opinions delivered straight to your inbox as soon as they’re published by visiting <a href="/emails"><strong>dor.sc.gov/emails</strong></a> and subscribing to the <strong>Tax Policy Updates</strong> mailing list.</p></blockquote><p>The SCDOR will continue to share information about South Carolina’s new Income Tax structure throughout 2027 tax season. Taxpayers can plan ahead now by visiting <a href="/iit" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="dcc95518-4da9-400e-886f-e7299e9af56f" data-entity-substitution="canonical" title="Individual Income Tax"><strong>dor.sc.gov/iit</strong></a>.</p><p>Follow the SCDOR on <a href="https://www.instagram.com/thescdor/"><strong>Instagram</strong></a>, <a href="https://www.facebook.com/theSCDOR"><strong>Facebook</strong></a>, <a href="https://x.com/theSCDOR"><strong>X</strong></a>, and <a href="https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCTBYoFDeMHjTGv9miVqlx8g/videos"><strong>YouTube</strong></a>, and subscribe to <a href="https://visitor.r20.constantcontact.com/d.jsp?llr=7rd5lgsab&amp;p=oi&amp;m=1119052828220&amp;sit=4kl6abgjb&amp;f=6b3dafe2-1c05-48d8-bcd4-ca21e25b9291"><strong>Reve</strong><em><strong>News</strong></em></a> for the latest updates, resources, and reminders.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>###</strong></p><h5>MEDIA INQUIRES:&nbsp;</h5><p><strong>SCDOR Public Information Office&nbsp;</strong></p><p><a href="mailto:news@dor.sc.gov"><strong>news@dor.sc.gov</strong></a></p></div>
